 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What is Duchenne, in simple terms?",
   "text": "Duchenne happens because of changes in a gene called DMD, which is the body's instructions for making a protein called dystrophin [E569d7fc, Eacae766]. A paper reports that when the dystrophin protein is missing, muscle fibers get damaged over and over, the body reacts with ongoing swelling and irritation (inflammation), scar-like tissue called fibrosis builds up, and the muscle's own repair cells stop working well [E0233056]. Scientists are still working to understand exactly how dystrophin normally works and what goes wrong without it [E456e501]. A paper also reports that in muscles without dystrophin, a chemical messenger called nitric oxide behaves differently, which turns on certain helper proteins called HDACs that change how genes switch on and off [E0077550]. The DMD gene's instructions are also used by teams of proteins called pathways, including ones for building the connections between muscle cells and their surroundings, for muscles tightening and moving, and for forming a group of proteins called the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex [Eaf57f8a, Ec463676, Ecd48059]. According to HPO, a database of symptoms seen in diseases, children with Duchenne can have a waddling way of walking [E655eff4], slower progress with movement skills such as sitting up or walking [E6a19111], a breathing pattern called a restrictive ventilatory defect [E5e04d4f], shallow or slow breathing called hypoventilation [Ed1216c8], times when breathing needs extra help called respiratory failure [Eebeda07], and sometimes mild intellectual disability [Ec98906d].",
